    "data": {
      "A": "Start from the fastest measured candidate (2dc99cd8, 0.172998 s). Seek low-risk inner-force reductions while retaining ascending j accumulation and the existing static outer OpenMP decomposition: hoist target coordinates, qualify non-aliasing/read-only accesses where valid, and test modest source-loop unrolling only if output remains correct. Do not adopt the slower 16-target tiling or full state-layout conversion without a measured win.",
      "B": "Investigate cross-target reuse with very small target blocks (for example 2–4), preserving each target’s j-order and force arithmetic. Compare AoS and only minimal position-array variants; the measured full SoA design is already marginally slower and the 16-target blocked design is substantially worse, so neither is a presumed base. Keep only independently validated improvements.",
      "C": "Focus on OpenMP execution structure and overhead around the proven outer-i parallelism: compare the best separate static parallel loops with a persistent team/orphaned worksharing form, correct required barriers, and small-n overhead behavior. Measurements show the two existing forms are effectively tied, so treat this as a careful confirmation/tuning path, not an assumed gain; avoid changing numerical reduction order.",
      "G": "Integrate from candidate 2dc99cd8 unless a new candidate is individually faster and correct. Benchmark each candidate and any merge independently; do not assume gains compose. Preserve binary I/O, ten-step force-then-integrate dependencies, and serial per-body ascending-j accumulation semantics. Reject the known slower 16-target tiling and any combination that regresses timing or correctness.",
      "analysis": "Baseline is 0.672326 s; simple static OpenMP outer-loop worksharing is best at 0.172998 s (~3.89x). Persistent-team, SoA, and position-array variants cluster slightly slower, while 16-target blocking is clearly slower (0.219170 s). Further work should therefore diversify around low-risk inner-loop/code-generation and small-block hypotheses, with integration selected solely by measured whole-program results."
